alt=3D""=20
            =
src=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/library/images/support/kbgraphics/pub=
lic/en-us/uparrow.gif">Back=20
            to the top</A></P>
            <H3 id=3DtocHeadRef>Usage Recommendations</H3>
            <SCRIPT=20
            type=3Dtext/javascript>loadTOCNode(2, =
'moreinformation');</SCRIPT>
            <B>NOTE</B>: Microsoft recommends that you follow these=20
            recommendations closely when you use junction points:=20
            <TABLE class=3D"list ul">
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Use NTFS ACLs to protect junction =
points from=20
                  inadvertent deletion.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Use NTFS ACLs to protect files and =
directories=20
                  that are targeted by junction points from inadvertent =
deletion=20
                  or other file system operations.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Never delete a junction point by using=20
                  Explorer, a del /s command, or other file system =
utilities=20
                  that walk recursively into directory trees. These =
utilities=20
                  affect the target directory and all =
subdirectories.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Use caution when you apply ACLs or =
change file=20
                  compression in a directory tree that includes NTFS =
junction=20
                  points.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Do not create namespace cycles with =
NTFS or DFS=20
                  junction points.</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD class=3Dbullet>=E2=80=A2</TD>
                <TD class=3Dtext>Put all your junction points in a =
secure=20
                  location in a namespace where you can test them out in =
safety,=20
                  and where other users will not mistakenly delete them =
or walk=20
                  through them.</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
            <P class=3DtopOfPage><A=20
            href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/205524#top"><IMG =
alt=3D""=20
            =
src=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/library/images/support/kbgraphics/pub=
lic/en-us/uparrow.gif">Back=20
            to the top</A></P>
            <H3 id=3DtocHeadRef>Feature Comparison to DFS</H3>
            <SCRIPT=20
            type=3Dtext/javascript>loadTOCNode(2, =
'moreinformation');</SCRIPT>
            NTFS junction points are similar to the junction points in =
DFS=20
            because both are tools that are used to graft storage =
namespaces=20
            together. However, DFS junction points typically have more =
features=20
            than NTFS junction points. The following table lists some of =
the=20
            differences between DFS and NTFS junction points.<BR><BR>
            <TABLE class=3Dtable cellSpacing=3D1>
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TH>Feature</TH>
                <TH>DFS Junction Points</TH>
                <TH>NTFS Junction Points</TH></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Junction point origin</TD>
                <TD>Local network share</TD>
                <TD>Local NTFS directory</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Junction point target</TD>
                <TD>Any network share</TD>
                <TD>Any valid Windows 2000 local path</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Recoverable</TD>
                <TD>Yes</TD>
                <TD>Yes (ChkDsk)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Persistable/portable state</TD>
                <TD>Yes (Save as File)</TD>
                <TD>Yes (explicit/copy/move/backup)</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Multi-targeting</TD>
                <TD>Yes</TD>
                <TD>No</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>API</TD>
                <TD>Yes</TD>
                <TD>Yes</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Graphical Tools</TD>
                <TD>Yes</TD>
                <TD>Minimal</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD>Availability</TD>
                <TD>NT Server 4.0 and Later</TD>
                <TD>Windows 2000 w/NTFS</TD></TR>
              <TR>
                <TD></TD>
                <TD></TD>
                <TD></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR><BR>For additional =
information=20
            about the support of NTFS junction points on a cluster, =
click the=20
            following article number to view the article in the =
Microsoft=20
            Knowledge Base:=20
            <DIV class=3Dindent><A class=3DKBlink=20
            =
href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/262797/">262797</A><SPAN=20
            class=3DpLink> =
(http://support.microsoft.com/kb/262797/)</SPAN>=20
            Reparse point support in Windows 2000-based clusters </DIV>
            <P class=3DtopOfPage><A=20
            href=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/kb/205524#top"><IMG =
alt=3D""=20
            =
src=3D"http://support.microsoft.com/library/images/support/kbgraphics/pub=
lic/en-us/uparrow.gif">Back=20
            to the top</A></P></DIV></DIV>
